<h1>Exercise Name: Low-to-High Cable Cable Fly (Low Cable Crossover)</h1>
<u><b><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></b></u>
This 3D anatomical visualization demonstrates Low-to-High Cable Chest Flyes (Low Cable Crossovers). The video highlights how placing the pulleys at the bottom position creates an upward cable angle that directly aligns with the muscle fibers of the upper chest (Pectoralis Major — Clavicular Head), maximizing tension on the clavicular pec head.
 Exercise Name: Low-to-High Cable Cable Fly (Low Cable Crossover)
 Target Muscles: Upper Chest (Pectoralis Major — Clavicular Head)
 Secondary Muscles: Anterior Deltoids (front shoulders), Upper Abs/Core (for stability)
</div></div>

<pre>
<h1>How to Perform (Step-by-Step)    </h1>

 <u><b><h4>1.Setup</h4></b></u>: Set the pulleys on a dual cable crossover station to the lowest position. Attach single D-handles to each side. Grab a handle in each hand with your palms facing forward or slightly upward.
 <u><b><h4>2.Stance</h4></b></u>: Step forward to bring the weights off the stack, placing one foot ahead of the other in a staggered stance for stability. Keep your chest up and shoulders pulled back.
 <u><b><h4>3.Fly Phase (Concentric)</h4></b></u>: Exhale as you bring your hands upward and inward in a sweeping arc, guiding the handles toward eye or chest level until your hands almost meet in front of your upper chest. Keep a slight, fixed bend in your elbows.
 <u><b><h4>4.Peak Squeeze</h4></b></u>: Pause for a split second at the top position, squeezing your upper chest forcefully.
 <u><b><h4>5.Return Phase (Eccentric)</h4></b></u>: Inhale as you slowly lower the handles back down along the same path under full control until you feel a comfortable stretch across your upper pecs.



<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉Upper Chest Focus:</b> Pulling upward against low-positioned resistance directly isolates the upper pectoral fibers better than flat flyes.
 <b>◉Constant Tension:</b> Cables provide smooth, continuous resistance across the entire movement, including the fully contracted peak.
 <b>◉Reduced Shoulder Strain:</b> The sweeping upward path is often more comfortable on the shoulder joints than heavy incline bench pressing.



<h1>Recommended Sets & Reps</h1>

| Goal | Sets | Reps | Rest |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Muscle Building (Hypertrophy) | 3 – 4 | 10 – 12 | 60 – 90 seconds |
| Endurance / Definition | 3 – 4 | 12 – 15 | 45 – 60 seconds |
| Strength | 3 – 4 | 8 – 10 | 90 seconds |

Keep your elbows locked at a slight angle throughout the motion—do not press or push with your triceps.
